A||||7||87FieroGT||Time for a new paint job.
Z||000000||87FieroGT||08-31-99||10:36 PM||||I will be doing a ZR2 conversion on my car toward the end of the year and I would like to know what the best way to paint the car is. Should I just do a BC or a BC/CC? Please help. I will be painting this myself. Please E-Mail me at fieroman@quixnet.net or I will look at this forum. Thanks.
Z||000001||ChadMan||09-01-99||07:34 AM||||My father-in-law painted his 86 SE himself.  It looks great (factory silver again).  He used a basecoat with no clearcoat, but applied three coats.  He bought his paint at NAPA and paid extra for some additive to make the paint semi-flexible, so it won't crack if (or should I say when) some dip$hit opens his/her door into the Fiero.<p>I questioned his choice to skip the clearcoat, but it sure looks nice.
Z||000002||Cooter||09-01-99||11:24 AM||||For a "solid" color- non metallic- use a good quality acrylic enamal single stage paint. For metallic colors, a base coat/clear coat will usually give a more even color and shine. The reason for this is because to keep the metallics from streaking, you must spray a "dust coat" to even them out. In single stage paint- a dust coat is also called dry spray and it leaves the paint really rough looking and feeling. With the clear coat, it doesn't matter how dry the base is sprayed because the clear goes on wet and dries slick enough to cover the rough texture of the base coat. Also, just spraying base coat without clear will not work, the composition of the paint makes it necessary to have the clear for shine and protection. There is a big difference between single stage and BC/CC paints!
Z||000003||batboy||09-01-99||04:45 PM||||Cooter, is there less prep work (read cheaper) if you get your car repainted back to the original color, or is the amount of prep work the same regardless what color you get it painted? My latest Fiero has the clear coat peeling and the black paint is faded just on the hood, truck, and roof. I'm tempted to have the top half of the car painted a new snazzy color that would go well with black. Would this be easy for a body shop to do and if so, would it cost less than a full paint job? I'd think it would involve less prep work.
Z||000004||Cooter||09-01-99||08:46 PM||||One thing that we have done on cars with peeling clear was to strip the damaged clear and prep those areas to be painted and sand the rest of the car with 1000 grit sandpaper. Then apply new base coat to the repaired areas and "blend" the base into the rest of the car. Then the whole car is cleared so it gives the appearance of a totally new paint job-because for the most part, it really is. It saves a good bit on base coat, but the amount of clear is the same as an overall paint job. This process is the big advantage of BC/CC paints- you can put the base only where you need it, if the majority of the paint is still in good shape just light sand it, put a light coat of base down and clear over it. That is how we did my Honda and it still looks great after 2 years.
Z||000005||DJRice||09-02-99||07:02 AM||||Cooter, what is the best way to strip the old  peeling clear coat off.  I have never tried body work before, but I am getting the urge to give it a shot to try to save $$$.
Z||000006||kornholio||09-02-99||02:28 PM||||What's the difference between clear coat and base coat?  How do I tell what I have.  Mine is a black 86 SE that has never had a paint job.
Z||000007||Cooter||09-02-99||07:04 PM||||DJ- I usually use 180 grit DA sandpaper on an air sander to sand the roughest spots of clear off and finish it out with 240 grit. Another option that you can use: Because of the fiero's construction, you could start with 240 wet/dry paper and "water sand" the clear away. I just keep a steady flow of water over the area you are sanding. The wet/dry paper will last a long time if you keep it wet and dont let it load up with sanding residue. After sanding the area smooth, three coats of high quality primer should get you in pretty good shape. Just dont make the "rookie" mistake of letting the primer spray onto areas that are not sanded! The primer will not stick and will cause paint failure when the car gets painted.<p>Kornholio- One way to see what paint you have is to find the GM options sticker in the car. I think it is under the front hood. At the bottom will be a code - usually WA with some numbers. It could also have the code: BC/CC that designates the base/clear paint. Another way is to put some rubbing compound on a rag and polish a section of the paint. If the rag turns to the color of the car, you either have a single stage paint job or have no clear left on your car.
